menu "sys_uart"

    config SYS_UART_TASK_PRIO
        int "RX task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24
        help
          Priority of the task that reads the console UART (and USB-Serial-JTAG)
          and turns "#R:" hex lines into router frames.

    config SYS_UART_TASK_STACK
        int "RX task stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        range 2048 16384
        help
          The line and frame buffers are static, not on this stack.

    config SYS_UART_DRIVER_RX_BUF
        int "Driver RX buffer (bytes)"
        default 1024
        range 256 8192
        help
          RX buffer of the UART (and USB-Serial-JTAG) driver installed on the
          console. Must be larger than the UART hardware FIFO (128 bytes).

    config SYS_UART_TX_QUEUE
        int "TX queue (bytes)"
        default 4096
        range 1024 32768
        help
          Frames waiting for the TX task (router answers and stream frames);
          each takes its length + ~8 bytes. A frame waits up to 1 s for room,
          then is dropped (ERR_UART_TX_FULL). Multiple of 4.

    config SYS_UART_TX_TASK_PRIO
        int "TX task priority"
        default 2
        range 1 24
        help
          The task that writes the queued frames as "#R:" lines. Low: the
          console is slow, and nothing waits for it but the queue.

    config SYS_UART_TX_TASK_STACK
        int "TX task stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        range 2048 16384

    config SYS_UART_SEND_WAIT_MS
        int "Send wait (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 10 10000
        help
          How long a frame (an answer or a stream frame alike) waits for room
          in the TX queue, then ERR_UART_TX_FULL. A stream frame waiting stalls
          the connector task: its own queue fills and the producer is refused
          (and sends again, as the telemetry sampler does), instead of losing
          the frame here unseen. No producer waits: sys_data_connector_send never
          blocks.

endmenu
